1. Introduction

The standard evolutionary account of human origins emphasizes mutation, selection, drift, migration, and demography. This account is necessary but incomplete. Human populations do not merely inhabit environments; they construct languages, norms, institutions, agricultural systems, cities, energy infrastructures, and computational networks. These constructions alter the selective environment and therefore alter biological evolution itself.

The resulting process is coupled. Genes affect cognition and behavior; cognition and behavior produce culture; culture produces technology; technology transforms environments; transformed environments impose new selection pressures; institutions coordinate collective action; computational systems accelerate the cycle.

The central scientific question is therefore not only

\text{“How did humans evolve biologically?”}

but also

\text{“How does a human-derived adaptive system evolve once biological, cultural, technological, institutional, and artificial-intelligence processes become coupled?”}

The first question concerns history. The second concerns future viability.

This revised manuscript proposes a formal framework in which biological evolution, cultural evolution, technological evolution, environmental transformation, institutional coordination, resilience, and machine intelligence are modeled as interacting stochastic dynamical processes. The objective is not to maximize any single component, but to maintain the coupled state within a viability region over long time horizons.

This revision incorporates critical advice from prior assessment. It now provides explicit provisional viability constraints, distinguishes deterministic and stochastic viability more carefully, reformulates the adaptation-time condition in terms of first-passage quantiles, operationalizes the AI oversight ratio, and adds common-shock structure to the multiplanetary extension. It also states explicitly that no viability kernel is computed and that the framework remains architectural.

