2. State Variables and Provisional Viability Constraints

The civilization-scale state vector is

X(t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
G(t)\\
C(t)\\
T(t)\\
E(t)\\
I(t)\\
R(t)\\
A(t)
\end{bmatrix}.

The components are defined operationally enough to permit future measurement and computation, but they are not yet all measured at civilization scale.

2.1 Biological state G(t)

G(t) is the biological-genetic state of the population, approximated as a vector of allele frequencies,

G(t)=\{p_1(t),p_2(t),\ldots,p_n(t)\}.

Polygenic trait values may be represented separately as

P(t)=W G(t),

where W is a weighting matrix linking genetic variants to traits such as lactase persistence, altitude adaptation, pathogen resistance, or metabolic efficiency.

2.2 Cultural-information state C(t)

C(t) is a distribution over cultural variants,

C(t)=\{c_1(t),c_2(t),\ldots,c_m(t)\},

where each c_i may represent a norm, technique, theory, language feature, institution, or practice. To avoid double counting, C contains transmitted information affecting behavior; T contains physical or computational capabilities measured in physical units; I contains coordination capacity measured by decision speed, legal reliability, and network connectivity.

2.3 Technological state T(t)

T(t) is a vector of measurable technological capabilities:

T(t)=
\begin{bmatrix}
T_E(t)\\ T_C(t)\\ T_M(t)\\ T_R(t)\\ T_B(t)\\ T_S(t)
\end{bmatrix},

where T_E is energy capture, T_C is computation, T_M is manufacturing precision and output, T_R is robotics and automation, T_B is biotechnology, and T_S is space capability.

2.4 Environmental state E(t)

E(t) is a finite-dimensional representation of the physical, ecological, and climatic environment, including temperature, atmospheric composition, biodiversity, soil fertility, water availability, and pathogen burden.

2.5 Institutional state I(t)

I(t) represents institutional coordination capacity, approximated by governance effectiveness, information-processing capacity, legal reliability, network connectivity, collective decision-making speed, and enforcement capacity.

2.6 Resilience state R(t)

R(t) represents the capacity to absorb perturbations without catastrophic loss of capability. It is measured through recovery time, redundancy, buffer capacity, and modular isolation of failures.

2.7 AI and machine-culture state A(t)

A(t) represents the state of artificial intelligence systems that participate in cultural transmission, prediction, design, experimentation, monitoring, and correction. It is not a passive tool. It may generate variants, transmit norms, and influence selection pressures.

2.8 Provisional explicit viability constraints

A serious limitation of the previous draft was that the viability region was formal but not operationally specified. This revision introduces provisional explicit constraints for the reduced model and provisional placeholder constraints for the full model.

For the reduced model introduced in Section 3, the viability region is defined as

\mathcal V_{\mathrm{red}}
=
\left\{
(g,c,e)\in\mathbb R^3:
g\le g_{\max},\quad
e\le e_{\max},\quad
c\ge c_{\min}
\right\}.

The parameters g_{\max}, e_{\max}, and c_{\min} are provisional and must be estimated independently. They are not to be fitted to collapse events.

For the full model, provisional constraints include:

· G(t) remains within bounds compatible with demographic and functional continuity.
· C(t) maintains a minimum level of transmitted adaptive diversity.
· T(t) does not collapse below levels required for energy, food, and medical systems.
· E(t) remains within planetary-boundary-like thresholds.
· I(t) maintains enforcement, information-processing, and decision-speed above minimal thresholds.
· R(t) maintains recovery time below a critical threshold.
· A(t) remains aligned, contained, or corrigible within specified bounds.

These constraints are not empirically calibrated here. They are architectural placeholders. A central task of the proposed research program is to estimate them from independent data.

