5. Viability, Controlled Invariance, and Stochastic Survival

A civilization is viable if its state remains within a region compatible with long-term survival and functional continuity.

Let the civilizational viability region be

\mathcal V=\{X\in\mathbb R^n : h_i(X)\le 0,\ i=1,\ldots,m\}.

The active constraints at a boundary point X\in\partial\mathcal V are

I(X)=\{i: h_i(X)=0\}.

5.1 Deterministic viability kernel

The viability kernel is

\mathcal K=
\left\{
X_0:\ \exists u(t)\in U
\text{ such that }
X(t)\in\mathcal V\quad \forall t\ge 0
\right\}.

This is Aubin’s viability kernel. The standard deterministic controlled-invariance condition is as follows. Suppose F is Lipschitz, U is compact, and for every state X\in\partial\mathcal V, there exists a control u\in U such that

\nabla h_i(X)\cdot F(X,u,d)<0

for all active constraints i\in I(X) and all admissible disturbances d\in D. Then, in the deterministic limit, \mathcal V is controlled invariant: there exists a feedback policy u^*=\pi(X) such that

X(t)\in\mathcal V\quad \forall t\ge 0.

This is the contingent-cone/tangency condition of viability theory.

5.2 Stochastic and jump viability

For the full jump-diffusion system, boundary drift conditions are not sufficient. Jumps may carry the state outside \mathcal V even when the deterministic drift points inward.

Let

\tau_{\mathcal V} = \inf\{t\ge 0 : X(t)\notin \mathcal V\}

be the first exit time.

For a fixed horizon T, a state X_0 is (T,\varepsilon)-viable under policy \pi if

P_{X_0}^{\pi}\left(\tau_{\mathcal V}>T\right)\ge 1-\varepsilon.

The finite-horizon viability kernel is

\mathcal K_{T,\varepsilon}
=
\left\{
X_0:
\exists \pi
\text{ such that }
P_{X_0}^{\pi}(\tau_{\mathcal V}>T)\ge 1-\varepsilon
\right\}.

The infinite-horizon viability kernel is

\mathcal K_\infty
=
\left\{
X_0:
\exists \pi
\text{ such that }
P_{X_0}^{\pi}(\tau_{\mathcal V}=\infty)=1
\right\}.

This is stronger than merely requiring P(\tau_{\mathcal V}=\infty)>0. A policy that gives a 1\% chance of indefinite survival and a 99\% chance of immediate failure is not useful for civilizational viability.

The value function

V^*(X,t)
=
\sup_{\pi}
P_{X,t}^{\pi}\left(\tau_{\mathcal V}>T\right)

satisfies, formally, the Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman variational inequality

\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial t}
+
\sup_{u\in U}
\left\{
F(X,u,d)\cdot \nabla V^*
+
\frac12 \operatorname{tr}\left(\sigma(X)\sigma(X)^\top D^2 V^*\right)
+
\lambda(X)\int_{\mathbb R^q}
\left[
V^*(X+J(X,z),t)-V^*(X,t)
\right]\nu(dz)
\right\}
=0

in the interior of \mathcal V, with boundary condition

V^*(X,t)=0

for X\in\partial\mathcal V, and terminal condition

V^*(X,T)=1

for X\in\mathcal V.

The viability kernel can then be approximated by

\mathcal K_{T,\varepsilon}
=
\left\{
X: V^*(X,0)\ge 1-\varepsilon
\right\}.

This formulation makes the stochastic viability problem computationally tractable, at least in reduced state spaces.

No viability kernel is computed in this manuscript. The computational plan is specified in Section 9.

