6. Adaptation Time and First-Passage Time

The stochastic viability condition can be connected to an operational adaptation-time condition, but the simple condition \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}} is not a general necessary condition. It is a deterministic marginal condition derived under a simplified hitting-time model.

6.1 Deterministic nominal exit time

Consider a state X_0\in\mathcal V at distance

d_0=\operatorname{dist}(X_0,\partial\mathcal V)

from the viability boundary. Suppose a perturbation induces a drift of magnitude v_p directly toward the boundary. If no control is applied, the nominal exit time is

\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}=\frac{d_0}{v_p}.

Suppose the control system requires time \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}} to detect the perturbation, select a response, and begin reversing the drift. To avoid boundary crossing under the deterministic approximation, we require

d_0 - v_p \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}} > 0.

This gives

\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}.

This is a useful heuristic, but it assumes constant drift directly toward the boundary and no diffusion or jumps.

6.2 Stochastic first-passage condition

In a stochastic environment, the exit time \tau_{\mathcal V} is a random variable. The appropriate condition is not a point comparison but a quantile condition:

P_{X_0}^{\pi}\left(\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathcal V}\right)
\ge
1-\varepsilon.

Equivalently, \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}} must be smaller than the (1-\varepsilon)-quantile of the first-passage-time distribution to the viability boundary. This condition incorporates diffusion, jumps, and model uncertainty.

The deterministic condition \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}} can be viewed as the special case in which \tau_{\mathcal V} is treated as deterministic and v_p is constant. It should be reported only with a safety margin

\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}
<
\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}-\delta,

where \delta>0 accounts for diffusion, jump risk, and model uncertainty. The margin \delta should be estimated from the variance of the first-passage time, not chosen arbitrarily.

This reformulation addresses the concern that the adaptation-time condition was previously presented as a general necessary condition. It is now clearly labeled as a deterministic marginal special case, and the general stochastic condition is stated explicitly.

