7. AI-Augmented Adaptation and Oversight Timescales

Machine culture is already present. Recommender systems alter social learning. Chatbots transmit norms and technical knowledge. Machine-generated strategies propagate through human populations.

The adaptive loop becomes

G \leftrightarrow C \leftrightarrow T \leftrightarrow E \leftrightarrow I \leftrightarrow A.

7.1 AI state dynamics

We model A(t) as a fast adaptive state:

dA = F_A(A,C,T,I,u_A)\,dt + \sigma_A\,dW_A + \int J_A(A,z)\,\tilde N_A(dt,dz).

The AI state is not a passive tool. It may participate in variation generation, transmission, selection, prediction, design, experimentation, monitoring, and correction.

7.2 Operational definitions of oversight timescales

The previous draft defined the oversight ratio but did not operationalize the terms. This revision specifies them as follows.

Let \tau_{\mathrm{AI}} be the characteristic time from the proposal of a machine-generated policy or design to its autonomous execution or deployment. It includes proposal generation, internal validation, and actuation.

Let \tau_{\mathrm{oversight}} be the characteristic time from the detection of an unaligned or harmful action to the completion of a corrective intervention that reverses, contains, or disables that action.

These definitions are measurable in principle. For example, in a code-generation setting, \tau_{\mathrm{AI}} might be the time from prompt to merged code; \tau_{\mathrm{oversight}} might be the time from detecting a vulnerability to reverting or patching the deployment.

7.3 Oversight lag ratio

The AI oversight lag ratio is

\Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}
=
\frac{\tau_{\mathrm{oversight}}}{\tau_{\mathrm{AI}}}.

A necessary viability condition is

\tau_{\mathrm{oversight}} < \tau_{\mathrm{cat}}

and, if AI is allowed to act autonomously,

\tau_{\mathrm{AI}} < \tau_{\mathrm{cat}}.

If \tau_{\mathrm{oversight}}\gg \tau_{\mathrm{AI}}, the system may leave the viability region before correction is possible.

The previous draft proposed the cautious criterion

\Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}\le 1+\epsilon,

for small \epsilon>0, unless strong containment or formal alignment guarantees are present. This revision acknowledges that the threshold 1+\epsilon is not derived from first principles. It is a provisional policy heuristic. The proposed research program should calibrate the threshold through simulation by varying \Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}, jump intensity, and control authority, and by measuring changes in finite-horizon survival probability.

7.4 Civilizational intelligence

Civilizational intelligence is defined as the operation

\text{Sense}
\rightarrow
\text{Infer}
\rightarrow
\text{Predict}
\rightarrow
\text{Act}
\rightarrow
\text{Measure}
\rightarrow
\text{Learn}
\rightarrow
\text{Correct}.

Let the adaptive operator be

\mathcal A:
(X_t,d_t)
\mapsto
(X_{t+1},\hat d_{t+1}).

Civilizational intelligence increases when

|\hat d-d|\rightarrow 0

and when the resulting control action reduces future deviation from the viable state.

