8. Multiplanetary Extension with Common Shocks

A planetary civilization is constrained by one environment. A multiplanetary civilization possesses multiple partially independent environmental compartments.

Let X_i(t) be the state of settlement i. For N settlements,

\mathbf X=(X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_N).

The coupled system is

\dot X_i
=
F_i(X_i,u_i,d_i)
+
\sum_{j\ne i}
K_{ij}(X_j,X_i,\Delta_{ij}),

where \Delta_{ij} is communication delay.

8.1 Global viability region

For each settlement,

X_i(t)\in\mathcal K_i.

The civilization as a whole requires

\mathbf X(t)\in\mathcal K_{\mathrm{civil}}.

The global viability region is generally not the product of local viability regions. Coupling, resource dependencies, communication delays, and correlated risks make the global viability region smaller than the product of local viability regions.

8.2 Common-shock structure

The earlier draft used marginal mutual information between settlement failure indicators. This is insufficient because settlements may share common causes even when their marginal failure probabilities are conditionally independent.

This revision introduces a latent common-shock process Z(t) representing shared macro risks, such as common technological failure modes, shared AI systems, common economic shocks, or correlated existential hazards. The failure indicators F_i and F_j are modeled as conditionally dependent given Z.

The appropriate decorrelation measure is not only marginal mutual information I(F_i;F_j), but conditional mutual information

I(F_i;F_j\mid Z).

A robust multiplanetary architecture should satisfy

I(F_i;F_j\mid Z)<\delta

for a meaningful fraction of settlement pairs. Equivalently, conditional on common shocks,

P(F_i\cap F_j\mid Z)
\le
(1+\epsilon)\,P(F_i\mid Z)P(F_j\mid Z)

for small \epsilon>0.

The failure covariance matrix is

\Sigma_F
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\operatorname{Cov}(F_i,F_j)
\end{bmatrix}_{i,j=1}^N.

A robust multiplanetary architecture should seek not merely a large number of settlements N, but low structural dependence after accounting for common shocks:

\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_{F\mid Z})
\le
\eta

or

\rho_{\max}(\Sigma_{F\mid Z})
\le
\rho_0.

This is a quantitative measure of risk decorrelation after removing common-cause effects.

8.3 Existential hazard rate

Civilizational survival can be modeled with a time-varying hazard rate \lambda(t). The probability of survival to time t is

S(t)=\exp\left(-\int_0^t \lambda(s)\,ds\right).

The adaptation condition \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}} corresponds to requiring that the control system reduce \lambda(t) before a high-hazard interval is entered.

