10. Proposed Research Program

We propose a five-layer research program. Each layer produces a publishable component and addresses a falsifiable sub-hypothesis. This manuscript reports no completed experimental or computational implementation of these layers.

Layer I: Genetic Adaptation and Cultural Niche Construction

Objective: Estimate \tau_G for specific loci and test whether cultural practices precede genetic adaptation.

Methods: Use ancient DNA datasets to track allele-frequency changes. Compute selection coefficients using full-likelihood methods such as CLUES2. Correlate allele-frequency change with archaeological cultural transitions.

Falsifiable hypothesis: Cultural transitions precede measurable increases in selection on associated genetic variants.

Layer II: Cultural Transmission and Open-Endedness

Objective: Formalize the condition for cumulative cultural adaptation and test it against open-endedness.

Methods: Use dual-inheritance models and agent-based simulations. Distinguish high-fidelity cumulative change from open-ended generation of new variants. Allow network-level dynamics to substitute for individual-level high fidelity.

Falsifiable hypothesis: High transmission fidelity alone is insufficient to explain human cultural uniqueness; open-endedness is required.

Layer III: Technological Adaptation Timescale

Objective: Measure \tau_T and estimate \tau_{C+T} for multiple technological classes.

Methods: Fit logistic diffusion curves to historical technology adoption data. Estimate \tau_T as time from 10\% to 90\% adoption. Compute parallel, max, and min combinations.

Falsifiable hypothesis: For multiple technological classes,

\tau_T\ll\tau_C\ll\tau_G.

Layer IV: Viability Kernel Estimation for Human–Environment Systems

Objective: Compute a reduced viability kernel and test the viability condition.

Methods: Use the reduced state z=(g,c,e). Implement controlled-invariance and finite-horizon survival algorithms using established viability-kernel toolboxes and level-set methods. Estimate the viability kernel under parameter uncertainty. Define a Lyapunov-like viability function

V(z)=\operatorname{dist}\bigl(z,\partial\mathcal K_{T,\varepsilon}\bigr).

Test whether historical civilizations approached the viability boundary before collapse.

Falsifiable hypothesis: Collapse events are preceded by approach to the viability-kernel boundary, using independent kernel estimates.

Computational plan: The finite-horizon stochastic viability kernel can be approximated by solving the HJB variational inequality using level-set methods or by using viability-kernel libraries such as VIABLAB. The reduced three-dimensional model is a feasible target for such computation. This manuscript does not report such computation.

Layer V: Multiplanetary and Machine-Augmented Simulation

Objective: Evaluate whether distributed settlements increase long-term survival probability.

Methods: Simulate N=2 settlements with communication delay. Include correlated hazards, shared supply chains, and a latent common-shock process. Include an AI state. Estimate joint failure probabilities, conditional mutual information I(F_i;F_j\mid Z), and conditional failure covariance \Sigma_{F\mid Z}.

Falsifiable hypothesis: Multiplanetary expansion increases survival probability only when settlement failure modes are sufficiently decorrelated after accounting for common shocks.

---

11. Discussion and Limitations

This framework has several limitations.

First, it does not provide a complete numerical model. The full seven-dimensional state is not yet tractable for direct viability-kernel computation. The reduced three-dimensional model is specified, but no viability kernel is computed in this manuscript.

Second, the explicit viability constraints are provisional. They are architectural placeholders and are not empirically calibrated. If they are fitted to collapse events, the resulting predictions become circular.

Third, it does not solve the AI alignment problem. The oversight timescale is a necessary but not sufficient condition for safe machine-augmented adaptation. The threshold on \Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}} is provisional and must be calibrated through simulation.

Fourth, it does not prove that a viable policy exists. The viability-kernel framework identifies necessary conditions and computational targets, but it does not guarantee that a feedback policy can be found in practice.

Fifth, it does not replace empirical work. The proposed falsifiable predictions and research layers require data collection, estimation, and simulation that are not reported here.

Sixth, the major-transitions chain

\text{Genome}
\rightarrow
\text{Brain}
\rightarrow
\text{Language}
\rightarrow
\text{Culture}
\rightarrow
\text{Institutions}
\rightarrow
\text{Technology}
\rightarrow
\text{AI}
\rightarrow
\text{Multiplanetary Infrastructure}

is a historical and conceptual narrative, not a theorem derived from the dynamical model. It is useful as a framing device, but it should not be confused with a formal result.

Seventh, the parallel timescale combination is not universally valid. The proposed research should report alternative combinations and sensitivity analyses.

Eighth, the common-shock model for multiplanetary risk is not fitted to any data. The conditional mutual information and conditional covariance metrics are formal targets, not yet empirical measures.

The value of the framework is architectural. It shows where measurements should be made, what conditions must be checked, and what failure modes must be avoided.

---

12. Conclusion

Homo sapiens did not cease evolving when civilization began. Evolution acquired additional channels.

Genes transmit biological information. Culture transmits behavioral and symbolic information. Institutions transmit organizational information. Technology externalizes cognition into physical systems. Artificial intelligence increasingly externalizes reasoning itself.

The resulting architecture is

G \leftrightarrow C \leftrightarrow T \leftrightarrow E \leftrightarrow I \leftrightarrow A.

The central claim is not that humans must biologically evolve into a superior species. It is that the next major evolutionary transition may occur primarily through the evolution of the adaptive systems surrounding the human organism.

This can be expressed as

\text{Genome}
\rightarrow
\text{Brain}
\rightarrow
\text{Language}
\rightarrow
\text{Culture}
\rightarrow
\text{Institutions}
\rightarrow
\text{Technology}
\rightarrow
\text{AI}
\rightarrow
\text{Multiplanetary Infrastructure}.

The central challenge is therefore

\text{Can Homo sapiens transform its biological, cultural, technological, institutional, and artificial-intelligence systems into a stable adaptive architecture capable of maintaining viability while expanding beyond Earth?}

The ultimate objective remains

\exists\,u(t)
\quad
\text{s.t.}
\quad
X(t)\in\mathcal K
\quad
\forall t\ge 0,

while simultaneously increasing the number of partially independent settlements and information channels.

No empirical data, physical tests, or trained models are reported in this manuscript. The framework is formal and architectural. The proposed research program is intended to convert it into a sequence of computable and falsifiable subproblems.

