Appendix A. Deterministic Viability and the Tangential Condition

Let the state space be \mathbb R^n, the control set U\subset\mathbb R^p be compact, and the disturbance set D\subset\mathbb R^q be compact. The deterministic controlled dynamics are

\dot X(t)=F(X(t),u(t),d(t)),

where F:\mathbb R^n\times U\times D\to\mathbb R^n is continuous and Lipschitz in X, uniformly in (u,d).

The viability region is

\mathcal V=\{X\in\mathbb R^n:h_i(X)\le 0,\ i=1,\ldots,m\},

where each h_i is C^1.

For X\in\partial\mathcal V, define the active constraint set

I(X)=\{i\in\{1,\ldots,m\}:h_i(X)=0\}.

Proposition A.1 (Deterministic controlled invariance criterion)

Suppose that for every X\in\partial\mathcal V, there exists a control u\in U such that

\nabla h_i(X)\cdot F(X,u,d)<0

for all i\in I(X) and all d\in D. Then \mathcal V is controlled invariant: there exists a feedback policy u^*=\pi(X) such that, for every initial condition X_0\in\mathcal V and every admissible disturbance trajectory d(\cdot),

X(t)\in\mathcal V\quad\forall t\ge 0.

Proof

The controlled system is equivalent to the differential inclusion

\dot X(t)\in F(X(t)):=\{F(X,u,d):u\in U,d\in D\}.

At each boundary point X\in\partial\mathcal V, the active constraints define the tangent cone

T_{\mathcal V}(X)
=
\left\{
v\in\mathbb R^n:
\nabla h_i(X)\cdot v\le 0
\text{ for all }i\in I(X)
\right\}.

The hypothesis states that for each X\in\partial\mathcal V, there exists f\in F(X) with

\nabla h_i(X)\cdot f<0

for all i\in I(X). Hence

F(X)\cap \operatorname{int} T_{\mathcal V}(X)\neq\emptyset.

In particular,

F(X)\cap T_{\mathcal V}(X)\neq\emptyset.

By the viability theorem for differential inclusions, there exists a solution X(\cdot) remaining in \mathcal V for all t\ge 0. The measurable selection theorem then provides a measurable feedback policy \pi(X(t))\in U realizing that solution. Therefore \mathcal V is controlled invariant. \square

This is the standard Aubin tangential condition. The strict inequality is stronger than necessary but sufficient and useful for robustness.

