Appendix B. Derivation of Timescale Formulas

B.1 Logistic selection and the time to fixation

For a single biallelic locus under additive selection with selection coefficient s, the deterministic allele-frequency dynamics are

\frac{dp}{dt}=s p(1-p),

where time is measured in generations.

Separating variables,

\int_{p_0}^{p_1}\frac{dp}{p(1-p)}
=
s\int_0^{\tau}dt.

The left-hand side is

\int_{p_0}^{p_1}\left(\frac1p+\frac1{1-p}\right)dp
=
\ln\frac{p_1(1-p_0)}{p_0(1-p_1)}.

Therefore,

\tau
=
\frac1s
\ln\frac{p_1(1-p_0)}{p_0(1-p_1)}.

For p_0=0.1 and p_1=0.9,

\tau
=
\frac1s
\ln\frac{0.9\cdot 0.9}{0.1\cdot 0.1}
=
\frac1s \ln 81.

In real time, with generation time g_T,

\boxed{
\tau_G
=
\frac{\ln 81}{s}\,g_T.
}

If multiple loci contribute, an effective selection coefficient s_{\mathrm{eff}} is used.

B.2 Logistic diffusion and adoption time

For a cultural trait or technology, let q(t) be the adoption fraction. The logistic diffusion model is

\frac{dq}{dt}
=
r q(1-q),

where r>0 is the diffusion rate. The same separation of variables gives the time from adoption fraction q_0 to q_1:

\tau(q_0,q_1)
=
\frac1r
\ln\frac{q_1(1-q_0)}{q_0(1-q_1)}.

For q_0=0.1 and q_1=0.9,

\tau
=
\frac1r \ln 81.

Thus,

\tau_C
=
\frac{\ln 81}{r_C},
\qquad
\tau_T
=
\frac{\ln 81}{r_T}.

B.3 Combination of cultural and technological timescales

The effective cultural-technological adaptation timescale is provisionally defined by the parallel combination

\tau_{C+T}^{-1}
=
\tau_C^{-1}+\tau_T^{-1}.

Hence

\tau_{C+T}
=
\frac{\tau_C\tau_T}{\tau_C+\tau_T}
\le
\min(\tau_C,\tau_T).

This formula is valid under the assumption that cultural and technological adaptation act as independent parallel channels with additive rates.

Because this assumption may fail, the proposed empirical program should report three alternatives:

· Parallel (additive rates):

\tau_{C+T}^{\mathrm{parallel}}
=
\frac{\tau_C\tau_T}{\tau_C+\tau_T}.

· Sequential (bottleneck):

\tau_{C+T}^{\mathrm{max}}
=
\max(\tau_C,\tau_T).

· Redundant (fastest channel dominates):

\tau_{C+T}^{\mathrm{min}}
=
\min(\tau_C,\tau_T).

The domain-specific ratio \Lambda^{(k)} should be computed under all three alternatives. The parallel formula is the most optimistic and should be treated as a lower bound on the effective timescale unless independent evidence supports channel independence.

