Appendix C. Stochastic Viability and Finite-Horizon Survival

C.1 Jump-diffusion dynamics

Let (\Omega,\mathcal F,\{\mathcal F_t\},\mathbb P) be a filtered probability space satisfying the usual conditions. The state evolves according to

dX(t)
=
F(X(t),u(t),d(t))\,dt
+
\sigma(X(t))\,dW(t)
+
\int_{\mathbb R^q} J(X(t-),z)\,\tilde N(dt,dz),

where:

· W is an \mathbb R^d-valued Brownian motion,
· \tilde N(dt,dz)=N(dt,dz)-\lambda(X(t-))\nu(dz)dt is a compensated Poisson random measure,
· \lambda(X) is the jump intensity,
· \nu(dz) is the jump-size distribution,
· J(X,z) is the jump amplitude.

The control u(t) may in principle affect F, \sigma, \lambda, and J, but in the present framework we primarily consider control through F.

The viability region is \mathcal V. The first exit time is

\tau_{\mathcal V}
=
\inf\{t\ge 0:X(t)\notin\mathcal V\}.

C.2 Finite-horizon survival value function

For a fixed horizon T>0, define

V^*(x,t)
=
\sup_{\pi}
P_{x,t}^{\pi}\left(\tau_{\mathcal V}>T\right),

where the supremum is over admissible feedback policies \pi such that u(t)=\pi(X(t)).

The terminal condition is

V^*(x,T)
=
1
\quad\text{for }x\in\mathcal V.

The boundary condition is

V^*(x,t)
=
0
\quad\text{for }x\notin\mathcal V.

In the interior of \mathcal V, the dynamic programming principle gives, formally,

V^*(x,t)
=
\sup_{u\in U}
\mathbb E\left[
V^*(X(t+dt),t+dt)
\,\mathbf 1_{\{\tau_{\mathcal V}>t+dt\}}
\right].

C.3 Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman variational inequality

Expand V^* around (x,t). For a small time increment dt, the infinitesimal generator of the jump-diffusion process applied to V^* is

\mathcal L^u V^*
=
F(x,u,d)\cdot \nabla V^*
+
\frac12 \operatorname{tr}\left(\sigma(x)\sigma(x)^\top D^2 V^*\right)
+
\lambda(x)
\int_{\mathbb R^q}
\left[
V^*(x+J(x,z),t)
-
V^*(x,t)
\right]
\nu(dz).

The dynamic programming principle yields

\sup_{u\in U}
\left[
\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial t}
+
\mathcal L^u V^*
\right]
=
0

in the interior of \mathcal V. Therefore,

\boxed{
\frac{\partial V^*}{\partial t}
+
\sup_{u\in U}
\left\{
F(x,u,d)\cdot \nabla V^*
+
\frac12 \operatorname{tr}\left(\sigma(x)\sigma(x)^\top D^2 V^*\right)
+
\lambda(x)
\int
\left[
V^*(x+J(x,z),t)-V^*(x,t)
\right]
\nu(dz)
\right\}
=
0.
}

This is a Hamilton–Jacobi–Bellman variational inequality for the survival-probability value function.

Remark on regularity. Under general state constraints, V^* is not necessarily C^1. The appropriate solution concept is a constrained viscosity solution or a discontinuous viscosity solution. The numerical approximation should use level-set methods, ordered upwind schemes, or viability-kernel algorithms designed for state-constrained problems.

C.4 Finite-horizon viability kernel

The finite-horizon viability kernel is

\mathcal K_{T,\varepsilon}
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal V:
V^*(x,0)\ge 1-\varepsilon
\right\}.

A state is (T,\varepsilon)-viable if there exists a policy under which the probability of remaining in \mathcal V until time T is at least 1-\varepsilon.

The infinite-horizon strict viability kernel is

\mathcal K_\infty
=
\left\{
x\in\mathcal V:
\exists \pi
\text{ such that }
P_{x}^{\pi}(\tau_{\mathcal V}=\infty)=1
\right\}.

This is stronger than the condition P(\tau_{\mathcal V}=\infty)>0. A policy with a small probability of indefinite survival and a large probability of rapid exit is not a useful viability criterion for civilization.

C.5 Boundary behavior under jumps

Jumps can carry the state outside \mathcal V even when the deterministic drift points strictly inward. In the integral term,

V^*(x+J(x,z),t)-V^*(x,t)

can be negative when x+J(x,z)\notin\mathcal V. This negative contribution reduces V^* and shrinks the viability kernel relative to the deterministic case. Therefore, deterministic controlled invariance is necessary but not sufficient for stochastic viability.

