Appendix D. Adaptation Time and First-Passage Time

D.1 Deterministic nominal exit time

Consider a state X_0\in\mathcal V at distance

d_0
=
\operatorname{dist}(X_0,\partial\mathcal V)
=
\inf_{Y\in\partial\mathcal V}
\|X_0-Y\|.

Suppose a perturbation at time t_0 induces a deterministic drift of magnitude v_p>0 directly toward the viability boundary. If no control is applied, the nominal distance at time t is

d(t)=d_0-v_p(t-t_0).

The nominal exit time is therefore

\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}
=
\frac{d_0}{v_p}.

Suppose the control system requires time \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}} to detect the perturbation, select a response, and begin reversing the drift. During the interval [t_0,t_0+\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}], the state continues to move toward the boundary. The distance at the end of the adaptation delay is

d(t_0+\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}})
=
d_0-v_p\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}.

To avoid crossing the boundary before control becomes effective, we require

d_0-v_p\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}>0.

Therefore,

\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}
<
\frac{d_0}{v_p}
=
\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}.

Hence,

\boxed{
\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}.
}

This is a deterministic marginal condition. It assumes constant drift directly toward the boundary and no diffusion or jumps.

D.2 Stochastic first-passage quantile condition

In a stochastic environment, the exit time \tau_{\mathcal V} is a random variable. The appropriate condition is not a point comparison but a quantile condition:

P_{X_0}^{\pi}\left(\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}<\tau_{\mathcal V}\right)
\ge
1-\varepsilon.

Equivalently, \tau_{\mathrm{adapt}} must be smaller than the (1-\varepsilon)-quantile of the first-passage-time distribution to the viability boundary.

This condition reduces to the deterministic condition when \tau_{\mathcal V} is treated as deterministic and v_p is constant. In general, it incorporates diffusion, jump risk, and model uncertainty.

D.3 Safety margin

In a stochastic environment, the deterministic condition should be replaced by

\tau_{\mathrm{adapt}}
<
\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}-\delta,

where \delta>0 accounts for diffusion, jump risk, and model uncertainty.

A first approximation to \delta can be derived from the variance of the first-passage time. If the first-passage-time distribution has mean \tau_{\mathrm{cat}} and standard deviation \sigma_{\tau}, then a quantile-based safety margin is

\delta
\approx
k\,\sigma_{\tau},

where k>0 is a confidence parameter. This margin should be estimated from the stochastic model, not chosen arbitrarily.

