Appendix F. AI Oversight Timescale and Risk Formulation

Let \tau_{\mathrm{AI}} be the characteristic time from the proposal of a machine-generated policy or design to its autonomous execution or deployment. It includes proposal generation, internal validation, and actuation.

Let \tau_{\mathrm{oversight}} be the characteristic time from the detection of an unaligned or harmful action to the completion of a corrective intervention that reverses, contains, or disables that action.

The AI oversight lag ratio is

\Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}
=
\frac{\tau_{\mathrm{oversight}}}{\tau_{\mathrm{AI}}}.

If \Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}\gg 1, machine-originated changes propagate faster than institutional correction.

Under the adaptation time condition of Appendix D, the relevant viability constraints are

\tau_{\mathrm{oversight}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}

and, if autonomous action is permitted,

\tau_{\mathrm{AI}}<\tau_{\mathrm{cat}}.

These are necessary but not sufficient.

The previous draft proposed the cautious criterion

\Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}
\le
1+\epsilon,

for small \epsilon>0, unless strong containment or formal alignment guarantees exist.

This threshold is not derived from first principles. It is a provisional policy heuristic. The proposed research program should calibrate the threshold through simulation by varying \Gamma_{\mathrm{oversight}}, jump intensity, and control authority, and by measuring changes in finite-horizon survival probability.

