Appendix G. Multiplanetary Risk Decorrelation with Common Shocks

Let F_i be the failure indicator of settlement i:

F_i=
\begin{cases}
1,&\text{if settlement }i\text{ fails},\\
0,&\text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

The marginal mutual information between failure events F_i and F_j is

I(F_i;F_j)
=
\sum_{a,b\in\{0,1\}}
P(F_i=a,F_j=b)
\log
\frac{P(F_i=a,F_j=b)}{P(F_i=a)P(F_j=b)}.

Marginal mutual information is insufficient if settlements share common causes. We therefore introduce a latent common-shock process Z(t) representing shared macro risks, such as common technological failure modes, shared AI systems, common economic shocks, or correlated existential hazards.

The conditional mutual information given Z is

I(F_i;F_j\mid Z)
=
\mathbb E_Z\left[
\sum_{a,b}
P(F_i=a,F_j=b\mid Z)
\log
\frac{P(F_i=a,F_j=b\mid Z)}
{P(F_i=a\mid Z)P(F_j=b\mid Z)}
\right].

A meaningful multiplanetary architecture should satisfy

I(F_i;F_j\mid Z)<\delta

for a threshold \delta, for a meaningful fraction of settlement pairs.

Equivalently, conditional on common shocks,

P(F_i\cap F_j\mid Z)
\le
(1+\epsilon)\,P(F_i\mid Z)P(F_j\mid Z)

for small \epsilon>0.

The failure covariance matrix conditional on Z is

\Sigma_{F\mid Z}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\operatorname{Cov}(F_i,F_j\mid Z)
\end{bmatrix}_{i,j=1}^N,

where

\operatorname{Cov}(F_i,F_j\mid Z)
=
\mathbb E[F_iF_j\mid Z]-\mathbb E[F_i\mid Z]\mathbb E[F_j\mid Z].

A robust multiplanetary architecture seeks not merely a large number of settlements N, but low structural dependence after accounting for common shocks:

\operatorname{tr}(\Sigma_{F\mid Z})
\le
\eta

or

\rho_{\max}(\Sigma_{F\mid Z})
\le
\rho_0.

These are quantitative risk-decorrelation metrics.

